{
  "id": "DrugBank_DB00201 - MONDO_6004",
  "verbalization": "Caffeine is implicated in the pathogenesis of Vasomotor Rhinitis through a series of indirect pathways. The evidence suggests that Caffeine may act as a mediator in the development of this condition by influencing inflammatory responses through multiple routes. Specifically, Caffeine is linked to Nasopharyngitis and Allergic Rhinitis, both of which are associated with Vasomotor Rhinitis via the indication of Potassium Iodide and Isoflupredone Acetate. These relationships imply that Caffeine could modulate the inflammatory cascade that leads to vasomotor symptoms, potentially through interactions with the immune system or neurogenic pathways. The presence of a common intermediate, Potassium Iodide, suggests a shared mechanism in the transition from nasal inflammation to Vasomotor Rhinitis. While the exact mechanism remains unclear, the integrated evidence points to Caffeine as a potential contributor to the condition through its influence on inflammatory and neurogenic pathways.",
